   $q$Read the sentences: "The weather balloon carried a small camera. It rose high above the clouds." Which choice combines them correctly using a relative clause?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The weather balloon carried a small camera, it rose high above the clouds.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice"},{"text":"The weather balloon, who carried a small camera, rose high above the clouds.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"who-for-thing"},{"text":"The weather balloon, which carried a small camera, rose high above the clouds.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The weather balloon which carried a small camera, rose high above the clouds.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unpaired-comma"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The weather balloon, which carried a small camera, rose high above the clouds.$q$, $q$"Which carried a small camera" is a relative clause about the balloon, set off by a pair of commas. "Who" is used for people, and the version with one comma separates the subject from its verb.$q$, 'draft', null)
